// Package phonetic defines how words turn into sound-alike keys and which encoder serves which language.
//
// An Encoder appends one or more keys for a word to a caller's slice. Language packages register their encoder at init with Register, and callers resolve one with For, which accepts a language or locale code ("de", "de-DE", "en_GB") and falls back to English. Importing a language package is what makes it available, so a binary carries only the languages it imports.
//
// English (Double Metaphone), German (Kölner Phonetik), French and Spanish have encoders. Italian has none and falls back to English, which already decides the corpus cases in the classes an Italian encoder would model (geminates, c and g before e and i, gn and gli, z and s, silent h, dropped accents); its Italian misses are real-word substitutions, splits, letter names, a number joined to a name and absent titles, which no spelling-to-sound rule reaches, and the Soundex and Metaphone families are built on English spelling. Add an Italian encoder only when a corpus shows Italian misses that sound-alike keys would fix. docs/src/content/docs/contributing/dependencies.md has the measurements behind each language's choice.
//
// Arabic keys words as the matcher writes them, in Latin letters, with Double Metaphone and two more keys: one merges the consonants Arabic dialects and speech recognition interchange (ج, ق and ك, the interdentals and sibilants, خ and ح), and one says ق as the glottal stop of Cairo.
//
// German keys with Kölner Phonetik, and this package's encoder keeps the library's codes. A German final "-er" is often heard as "-a" ("lieda" for Lieder, "es war somma"), which Kölner Phonetik keys apart (the r codes 7), so the matcher keys a German word of four or more letters ending in "-er" without its r (internal/core/match germanKeys) rather than this encoder doing so. That key replaces the encoder's rather than joining it as a second, as the English encoder's R-less key does, since a second key plays more absent titles; "-ern" is keyed as written. English loanwords and names ("skuter" for Scooter, "kraftklup") and c for k ("fitzkaraldo") are not a German rule's to chase.
//
// Every encoder follows one contract: the input is already folded (lowercase, diacritics stripped as match.Normalize does); a word made only of ASCII digits is its own key; a word that encodes to nothing is its own key, so a non-empty word never gets an empty key; there are at most three keys, primary first, without duplicates; keys other than those fallbacks are ASCII; and the encoder is stateless and safe for concurrent use.
package phonetic
